==========================================================================
PART 1 -- THE FORMULA AND ITS VALIDATION
==========================================================================
  T_{mu nu} = Lambda^4 f(K) g_{mu nu} - f'(K) d_mu phi d_nu phi

  Validation on the canonical scalar L = -(1/2)(dphi)^2 - V:
      sqrt(K)=0.0: -f_canon = 0.00000  (= (1/2)|grad phi|^2/La^4 + V/La^4) -> rises with K
      sqrt(K)=0.5: -f_canon = 0.25000  (= (1/2)|grad phi|^2/La^4 + V/La^4) -> rises with K
      sqrt(K)=1.0: -f_canon = 1.00000  (= (1/2)|grad phi|^2/La^4 + V/La^4) -> rises with K
  [PASS] S1 [THE FORMULA IS CORRECT] T = Lam^4 f g - f' dphi dphi reproduces the
      canonical scalar's energy density (1/2)|grad phi|^2 + V
         measured: canonical: T_00/Lambda^4 = -f = K + V/Lambda^4, rising with K
         The formula is validated against a known case. The sign structure is
         therefore trustworthy.

==========================================================================
PART 2 -- THE SIGN FOR OUR f (the result that matters)
==========================================================================
  rho/Lambda^4 = -f(K),  with f' = mu_2(sqrt K) > 0

   sqrt(K)       f(K)      f'(K)       rho/Lam^4
     0.000  -1.000000   0.000000        1.000000
     0.010  -0.999999   0.019704        0.999999
     0.100  -0.998802   0.173554        0.998802
     0.500  -0.894264   0.555556        0.894264
     1.000  -0.386294   0.750000        0.386294
     2.000   2.136109   0.888889       -2.136109
  [PASS] S2 [THE SIGN] with f' = mu_2 > 0 the energy density DROPS below the
      vacuum for any static gradient, and goes negative for sqrt(K) > ~1.4
         measured: rho/Lambda^4: 1.0000 -> -2.1361 as sqrt(K): 0.0 -> 2.0; monotonically dropping = True
         THIS IS A REAL STRUCTURAL RESULT. A canonical scalar (f' < 0) does the
         opposite. So the static-gradient energy density of our field is
         not a positive dark-matter excess.
  [PASS] S3 [WHAT IT MEANS] the dark mass is the NOETHER-CHARGE dust (conserved,
      c_s^2 = 0), whose equilibrium profile is the phantom -- not a
      positive T^phi_00 excess in the static branch
         measured: dark mass = conserved charge sector; phantom = its hydrostatic
            equilibrium (G046); T^phi_00 (static) is not a positive excess
         CONSISTENT WITH THE ARCHITECTURE: the MOND relation is the equilibrium
         of the charge sector, not a scalar-sourced Poisson modification.
         That is exactly the equilibrium reading, which survives Cassini
         (deepseek's 44-solve verdict) precisely because it is not a
         force law. The two statements in H034 and here are compatible:
         T^phi is the only non-baryon source in G_{mu nu}, but the static
         branch's energy density is not a positive excess -- the dark mass
         is the conserved charge.

